hat for\nL213@P2: F1 (1.000), a single stimulus cannot raise [Ca2+]cyt over the putative\nL214@P2: threshold Fmax and a second stimulus is necessary to meet the puta\u0002tive threshold for movement, at least in the lateral area.\nL215@P2: Next, we examined whether a second stimulus could raise [Ca2+]cyt\nL216@P2: to the threshold after the memory limit time of 30s after the first stim\u0002ulus2,3\nL217@P2: . We measured Ft every 0.02s after the time of F1 in the absence\nL218@P2: of a second stimulus (Fig. 3d,e). [Ca2+]cyt decreased with time in both\nL219@P2: lateral and central areas of the leaf and decay curves were well fit into\nL220@P2: the two-phase exponential curves with lower Akaike’s Information\nL221@P2: Criterion in comparison to other simpler models (Supplementary\nL222@P2: Fig. 4). Fres and Fmax in the separate experiments with the second\nL223@P2: stimulus were plotted on the same graph with showing whether\nL224@P2: the leaf closed or not at given time (Fig. 3d,e). Leaf closure depends\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCalcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap (https://im7.freeforumzone.it/up/72/65/1608312795.pdf)\nciteturn23view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last year; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view0\",\"lineno\":372}); Total lines: 817\nL347@P3: 8 10 0 2 4 6 0 15 30 45 60\nL348@P3: Time t (s) Time t (s)\nL349@P3: 0 15 30 45 60\nL350@P3: Time t (s)\nL351@P3: Time t (s)\nL352@P3: 8 10\nL353@P3: 1.5\nL354@P3: 2.0\nL355@P3: 1.0\nL366@P3: stimuli are indicated as 1st and 2nd, respectively. The leaf closed after the second stimulus. F1, maximum Ft\nL367@P3:  after the first stimulus before the second\nL368@P3: stimulus; Fres, Ft\nL369@P3:  immediately before the second stimulus; Fmax, maximum Ft after the second stimulus. Plots of fluorescence intensity without normalization\nL370@P3: are shown in Extended Data Fig. 7. c, Regression curves in the logistic regression analyses of Fres at lateral areas (leftmost), Fres at central areas (second\nL371@P3: left), Fmax at lateral areas (second right) and Fmax at central areas (rightmost). Leaf states (black dots: 1, closed; 0, open), regression curve (red lines), 95%\nL372@P3: CI of the regression curve (grey regions), predicted putative thresholds of Ft\nL373@P3:  to trigger closure of 50% leaves (solid blue lines) and 95% CI of the putative\nL374@P3: threshold (dashed blue lines) are shown. d,e, Decay curves of the fluorescence intensity (Ft\nL375@P3: ) after F1 is reached in the lateral (d) and central (e) area. The\nL376@P3: mean (black line) and standard deviation of the mean (grey region) are plotted every 0.02 s (n= 6, leaves from two biologically independent plants). Fres\nL377@P3: (magenta) and Fmax (green) in c are plotted. Leaves were closed (circles) or not (x). f, Regression curves in the logistic regression analyses of duration\nL378@P3: between the first and second stimuli. Leaf states (black dots: 1, closed; 0, open), regression curve (red lines), 95% CI of the regression curve (grey regions),\nL379@P3: predicted putative thresholds of Ft\nL380@P3:  to trigger closure of 50% leaves (solid blue lines) and 95% CI of the putative threshold (dashed blue lines) are shown.\nL381@P3: Table 1 | Putative thresholds of [Ca2+]cyt and duration with logistic regression analyses\nL382@P3: Explanatory variable Area Threshold (95% CI)a Odds ratio (95% CI)b Maximum likelihood Likelihood ratio test\nL383@P3: Fres Lateral 0.5058\nL384@P3: (0.3738–0.6598)\nL385@P3: 1.165 × 104 (1.32 × 102–1.145 × 107) 2.2 × 10−4 0.000002***\nL386@P3: Central 0.3756\nL387@P3: (0.2599–0.4999)\nL388@P3: 4.114 × 104\nL389@P3: (2.141 × 102–1.382 × 108)\nL390@P3: 9.023 × 10−5 0.000008***\nL391@P3: Fmax Lateral 1.327 (1.226–1.422) 7.627 × 104\nL392@P3: (1.558 × 102–7.536 × 108)\nL393@P3: 8.301 × 10−6 0.000044***\nL394@P3: Central 1.179 (1.026–1.364) 1.608 × 104 (0.932–3.825 × 109) 6.17 × 10−9 0.063311\nL395@P3: Duration – 30.88 s (21.4–45.42) 0.8917 (0.8093–0.9446) 3.229 × 10−4 0.000001***\nL396@P3: a\nL397@P3: Value of Fmax or Fres where half number of the trap leaves close. bEffect size calculated from the regression coefficient. a,b95% CI: predicted 95% confidence intervals of putative threshold. ***P< 0.001\nL398@P3: (n= 30).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCalcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap (https://im7.freeforumzone.it/up/72/65/1608312795.pdf)\nciteturn23view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last year; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view0\",\"pattern\":\"30 s\"}); Total lines: 817\nL0@P0: Letters\nL1@P0: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41477-020-00773-1\nL2@P0: 1\nL3@P0: National Institute for Basic Biology, Okazaki, Japan. 2Department of Basic Biology, The Graduate University for Advanced Studies (SOKENDAI), Okazaki,\nL4@P0: Japan. 3JST, PRESTO, Saitama, Japan. 4Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, Graduate School of Science and Engineering, Saitama\nL5@P0: University, Saitama, Japan. 5Department of Botany, University of Wisconsin, Madison, WI, USA. 6Institute for Molecular Plant Physiology and Biophysics,\nL6@P0: University of Würzburg, Würzburg, Germany. 7Department of Biology, Graduate School of Science, Kobe University, Kobe, Japan. 8Graduate School of\nL7@P0: Business Administration, Hitotsubashi University, Kunitachi, Japan. 9Zoology Department, College of Science, King Saud University, Riyadh, Saudi Arabia. 10School of Engineering, Utsunomiya University, Utsunomiya, Japan. ✉e-mail: mhasebe@nibb.ac.jp\nL8@P0: The leaves of the carnivorous plant Venus flytrap, Dionaea\nL9@P0: muscipula (Dionaea) close rapidly to capture insect prey. The\nL10@P0: closure response usually requires two successive mechanical\nL11@P0: stimuli to sensory hairs on the leaf blade within approximately\nL12@P0: 30 s (refs. 1–4). An unknown biological system in Dionaea is\nL13@P0: thought to memorize the first stimulus and transduce the\nL14@P0: signal from the sensory hair to the leaf blade2\nL15@P0: . Here, we link\nL16@P0: signal memory to calcium dynamics using transgenic Dionaea\nL17@P0: expressing a Ca2+ sensor. Stimulation of a sensory hair caused\nL18@P0: an increase in cytosolic Ca2+ concentration ([Ca2+]cyt) starting\nL19@P0: in the sensory hair and spreading to the leaf blade. A second\nL20@P0: stimulus increased [Ca2+]cyt to an even higher level, meeting a\nL21@P0: threshold that is correlated to the leaf blade closure. Because\nL22@P0: [Ca2+]cyt gradually decreased after the first stimulus, the\nL23@P0: [Ca2+]cyt increase induced by the second stimulus was insuf\u0002ficient to meet the putative threshold for movement after\nL24@P0: about 30 s. The Ca2+ wave triggered by mechanical stimula\u0002tion moved an order of magnitude faster than that induced by\nL25@P0: wounding in petioles of Arabidopsis thaliana5\nL26@P0:  and Dionaea. The\nL27@P0: capacity for rapid movement has evolved repeatedly in flower\u0002ing plants. This study opens a path to investigate the role of\nL28@P0: Ca2+ in plant movement mechanisms and their evolution.\nL29@P0: Plants have evolved a variety of mechanisms that ‘remember’\nL30@P0: external stimuli as part of numerous processes, including accli\u0002mation to harsh environments, systemic acquired resistance to\nL31@P0: pathogens and vernalization6\nL32@P0: . In the short-term memory system of\nL33@P0: Dionaea (Fig. 1a), the second stimulus can be to any of the leaf ’s six\nL34@P0: sensory hairs (Fig. 1b), regardless of which hair received the first\nL35@P0: stimulus2\nL36@P0: . Previous studies showed that mechanical stimulation of a\nL37@P0: sensory hair generates action potentials that propagate to both lobes\nL38@P0: of the leaf7,8\nL39@P0: . Other studies have shown that leaf closure is blocked\nL40@P0: by Ca2+ channel inhibitors that should inhibit increase of the cyto\u0002solic Ca2+ concentration ([Ca2+]cyt) of leaf cells, emphasizing the\nL41@P0: importance of Ca2+ in the response9–11. On the basis of these results,\nL42@P0: the mechanism of Dionaea memory has been proposed to be as fol\u0002lows10,12: (1) [Ca2+]cyt increases during excitation; (2) [Ca2+]cyt or the\nL43@P0: concentration of some Ca2+-activated regulatory molecules must\nL44@P0: reach a threshold for movement; (3) a single action potential is not\nL45@P0: enough to increase [Ca2+]cyt to the threshold and at least two action\nL46@P0: potentials are necessary to trigger movement; (4) [Ca2+]cyt decreases\nL47@P0: after the first stimulus and after 30 s does not reach the threshold\nL48@P0: even with a second stimulus. This hypothesis has not been directly\nL49@P0: tested because of the lack of a method for spatiotemporal calcium\nL50@P0: monitoring in Dionaea.\nL51@P0: Since transformation of Dionaea has not been reported, to\nL52@P0: observe spatiotemporal cytosolic [Ca2+] changes in Dionaea,\nL53@P0: we set out to transform excised leaves with Agrobacterium car\u0002rying a construct for constitutive expression of sGFP (refs. 13–15;\nL54@P0: pSB111U2-sGFP; Supplementary Fig. 1a). On the basis of the num\u0002ber of leaves expressing sGFP, etiolated leaves had a significantly\nL55@P0: higher transformation rate (97% (n=96)) than did non-etiolated\nL56@P0: leaves (46% (n=91)) (two-tail P=4.8×10–12 by Fisher’s exact\nL57@P0: test). Furthermore, shoot regeneration rates from etiolated leaves\nL58@P0: were significantly higher under continuous dark conditions (55%\nL59@P0: (n=60)) than under continuous light conditions (27% (n=60))\nL60@P0: (two-tail P=0.0028 by Fisher’s exact test). From 77 excised etiolated\nL61@P0: leaves, we obtained seven transgenic plants that constitutively and\nL62@P0: ubiquitously expressed sGFP (Supplementary Fig. 2).\nL63@P0: Using the same procedures, we next produced plants trans\u0002formed with a construct for constitutive expression of the calcium\nL64@P0: sensor protein GCaMP6f, which contains a cpEGFP fluorescent\nL65@P0: moiety14,16 (pSB111U2-GCaMP6f; Supplementary Fig. 1b). Five\nL66@P0: transgenic plants were obtained from 245 excised etiolated leaves.\nL67@P0: When non-excised leaves of GCaMP6f-transgenic Dionaea were\nL68@P0: placed on ice, cpEGFP fluorescence increased in both leaf lobes and\nL69@P0: petioles (Fig. 1c), as reported for cold-treated leaves of Arabidopsis\nL70@P0: thaliana17. Cytosolic localization of cpEGFP fluorescence was\nL71@P0: observed in a cut leaf blade (Supplementary Fig. 3).\nL72@P0: We mechanically stimulated a sensory hair of GCaMP6f Dionaea\nL73@P0: and observed that cpEGFP fluorescence increased within 0.02 s at\nL74@P0: the base of the hair (Fig. 2a), where the sensory cells that gener\u0002ate action potentials are located7\nL75@P0: . Over time, fluorescence intensity\nL76@P0: continued to increase in the sensory hair (Fig. 2a) and fluorescence\nL77@P0: spread radially from the sensory hair to the surrounding leaf tissue\nL78@P0: (Fig. 2c,d, Extended Data Fig. 1 and Supplementary Videos 1 and 2).\nL79@P0: There are non-pigmented glands and pigmented digestive glands on\nL80@P0: the leaf blade2\nL81@P0: . The increase of fluorescence intensity was detected\nL82@P0: in the former glands but not in the latter. Fluorescence also propa\u0002gated from one lobe of the leaf to the other (Fig. 2e,f left, Extended\nL275@P3: showed that the putative 50% leaf closure threshold for the duration\nL276@P3: of the F1 signal was 30.88s with 95% CI 21.4–45.42s (Fig. 3f and\nL277@P3: Table 1), which concurs with previous studies2,3\nL278@P3: . The means of F30.88\nL279@P3: in the lateral and central areas were 0.4631±0.07214 (mean±s.e.m.,\nL280@P3: n=6 leaves) (Fig. 3d) and 0.2638±0.07758 (mean±s.e.m., n=6\nL281@P3: leaves) (Fig. 3e), respectively, which are within the ranges of 95% CIs\nL282@P3: of putative Fres thresholds (Table 1). These results indicate that the\nL283@P3: time course of [Ca2+]cyt decay correlates with the duration of the sig\u0002nal produced by the first stimulus and that the 30-s length of mem\u0002ory is explained by the decay of [Ca2+]cyt.\nL284@P3: We also investigated whether the third stimulus can increase\nL285@P3: [Ca2+]cyt over the putative threshold Fmax and trigger movement,\nL286@P3: when the second stimulus does not trigger movement. After the\nL287@P3: third stimulus, Ft\nL288@P3:  at the lateral area reached the 95% CI of the\nL289@P3: putative threshold and the leaf closed (Extended Data Fig. 8 and\nL290@P3: Supplementary Fig. 5 and Supplementary Video 6). On the other\nL291@P3: hand, even when Ft\nL292@P3:  at central area reached the 95% CI of the\nL293@P3: putative threshold, the leaf did not close (Supplementary Fig. 5\nL294@P3: and Extended Data Fig. 8). These results are concordant with the\nL295@P3: result that Fmax at lateral area significantly correlates to the move\u0002ment but Fmax at central area does not (Table 1).\nL296@P3: To further investigate relationships between the putative [Ca2+]cyt\nL297@P3: threshold and movement, two experiments were used. We found\nL298@P3: that Dionaea leaves close with no mechanical stimuli within 1min,\nL299@P3: Lateral area\nL300@P3: F1 Ft\nL301@P3: Fres\nL302@P3: 2nd\nL303@P3: 2nd\nL304@P3: Leaf closed Leaf closed\nL305@P3: 1 1 st st\nL306@P3: Fmax\nL307@P3: Lateral area Lateral area\nL308@P3: Lateral area\nL309@P3: Green: Fmax\nL310@P3: Magenta: Fres\nL311@P3: Green: Fmax\nL312@P3: Magenta: Fres\nL313@P3: Central area\nL314@P3: Central area Central area\nL315@P3: Central area\nL316@P3: Standardized fluorescence intensity Closure possibility\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCalcium dynamics during trap closure visualized in transgenic Venus flytrap (https://im7.freeforumzone.it/up/72/65/1608312795.pdf)\nciteturn23view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last year; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 18; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn22view0\",\"pattern\":\"temperature\"}); Total lines: 817\nL473@P4: continuous darkness at 25 °C. The solid medium was exchanged once a week for\nL474@P4: at least a month, until transgenic shoots formed. One month after infection, the\nL475@P4: leaves were transferred to solid 1/2 MS medium plates lacking cefotaxime sodium\nL476@P4: salt. Transgenic shoots were cultivated under 8h light/16h darkness at 25 °C until\nL477@P4: green leaves formed and acclimated under 16h light/8h darkness at 22 °C in soil\nL478@P4: consisting of peat moss and perlite at a ratio of 2 to 1.\nL479@P4: Tissue clearing. Leaves were fixed in 20% (v/v) acetic acid (Wako) dissolved in\nL480@P4: ethanol (Wako) with four 15-min rounds of decompression to −0.08MPa in gauge\nL481@P4: pressure at room temperature and then kept overnight at 4 °C. Fixed leaves were\nL482@P4: rehydrated using a dilution series of ethanol (90%, 70%, 50%, 30% and 0%) and\nL483@P4: cleared in a solution consisting of 8 g of chloral hydrate (Wako), 1ml of glycerol\nL484@P4: (Wako) and 2ml of distilled water for 3d at room temperature under continuous\nL485@P4: dark conditions.\nL486@P4: Microscopy. Stereoscopic images and videos were taken using a SZX16 (Olympus)\nL487@P4: fluorescence microscope equipped with a SZX2-FGFP long-pass filter (Olympus)\nL488@P4: and a digital camera DP74 (Olympus). Fluorescence videos were taken using\nL489@P4: the linear mode of cellSens Standard (1.17) in a range where the fluorescence\nL490@P4: intensities of samples and brightness values of captured images are linear. All\nL491@P4: fluorescence movies were taken at 50 frames per second, with a 2-ms exposure\nL492@P4: time (Fig. 2a, b) or 15-ms exposure time (others) and 16× gain, in linear contrast\nL493@P4: mode and with 2×2 binning. Images were saved as a sequence of uncompressed\nL494@P4: tiff files. Blue, green and red channels were split from the original images and only\nL495@P4: green channel images were used for the analysis. All fluorescence images were\nL496@P4: pseudocoloured with green or LUT (fire lookup table) in ImageJ (1.52p). All videos\nL497@P4: were constructed from the green channel images of the original uncompressed\nL498@P4: tiff images. The time course of the two-dimensional propagation of increased\nL499@P4: fluorescence was determined by manually connecting the boundaries of fluorescent\nL500@P4: areas (Fig. 2d,e). To calculate the propagation velocity of the [Ca2+]cyt increase,\nL501@P4: leaves and petioles were considered to be flat planes and the velocity induced by\nL502@P4: the stimulus was measured 0.04 s (Extended Data Fig. 3a,c), 0.02 s (Extended Data\nL503@P4: Fig. 3b) and 5 s (Extended Data Fig. 3d) after the stimulus. To compare the areas\nL504@P4: with increased [Ca2+]cyt after application of the first and second stimuli, the pixel\nL505@P4: intensity distribution was classified into two distributions: pixels with large changes\nL506@P4: and pixels with small changes in intensity. Pixels with small changes were set as\nL507@P4: background and were assigned the same value in images acquired after the first and\nL508@P4: second stimulation. For quantitative analysis, two ROIs (regions of interest) were\nL545@P5: g tðÞ¼ aebt þ c ð2Þ\nL546@P5: g tðÞ¼ at þ b ð3Þ\nL547@P5: where g(t) is [Ca2+]cyt, t is time and a–e are parameters.\nL548@P5: Observation of digestive glands. Leaf lobes were cut by a razor blade, mounted to\nL549@P5: the 35-mm dish (Falcon) by double-sided tape and immersed for 0.5h in a buffer\nL550@P5: containing 0.1mM KCl (Wako), 10mM CaCl2 (Wako), 20mM sorbitol (Wako)\nL551@P5: and 5mM 2-(N-morpholino)ethanesulfonic acid (Dojindo Laboratories) adjusted\nL552@P5: with 2-amino-2-hydroxymethyl-1,3-propanediol (Wako) to pH6 (ref. 28). The most\nL553@P5: distal sensory hair was mechanically stimulated by a needle. Stereoscopic images\nL554@P5: and videos were taken using a SZX16 (Olympus) as mentioned above.\nL555@P5: La3+ treatment. Leaves were cut by scissors at the proximal end of the petioles.\nL556@P5: Since [Ca2+]cyt signals increased by wounding, the cut leaves were kept on a\nL557@P5: Petri dish for 3min at room temperature to decrease [Ca2+]cyt levels. Since some\nL558@P5: leaves do not respond to mechanical stimuli after the cut, the cut leaves were\nL559@P5: mechanically stimulated to confirm the elevation of [Ca2+]cyt levels by a mechanical\nL560@P5: stimulus. The stimulated leaves with increased [Ca2+]cyt levels were kept on a\nL561@P5: Petri dish for 3min to decrease the [Ca2+]cyt levels, then placed in the 35-mm dish\nL562@P5: (Falcon) and immersed in 8ml of water or 20mM LaCl3 (Wako) solution. A 0.1%\nL563@P5: (v/v) Silwet-L77 (BMS) solution was added to diminish the surface tension that\nL564@P5: stimulates trigger hairs to close leaves. After 30min of immersion, the liquid was\nL565@P5: removed. The treated leaves were kept without touching for 3min and then the\nL566@P5: sensory hair was mechanically stimulated. Stereoscopic images and videos were\nL567@P5: taken using a SZX16 (Olympus) as mentioned above.\nL568@P5: Reporting Summary. Further information on research design is available in the\nL569@P5: Nature Research Reporting Summary linked to this article.\nL570@P5: Data availability\nL571@P5: The data that support the findings of this study are available from the\nL572@P5: corresponding author upon reasonable request. Sequence data for genes and\nL573@P5: plasmids can be found in GenBank under the following accession numbers: sGFP\nL574@P5: (ABL09837), pSB1 (AB027255) and pSB11 (AB027256). Sequence of GCaMP6f can\nL575@P5: be found in Addgene data libraries as catalogue no. 40755. Source data are provided\nL576@P5: with this paper.\nL577@P5: Received: 16 February 2020; Accepted: 24 August 2020;\nL578@P5: Published: xx xx xxxx\nL579@P5: References\nL580@P5: 1. Macfarlane, J. M. Contributions t